- op: create  # 0f30
  at: '2026-09-12T17:35:09Z'  # 0f30
  by: agent  # 0f30
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 0f30
  clock: 1  # 0f30
  nonce: 0f30  # 0f30
  data:  # 0f30
    kind: invariant  # 0f30
    title: "tracker init has no actor guard and calls config_lock, so an agent run silently TAKES OWNERSHIP of the human-owned governance config"  # 0f30
    status: violated  # 0f30
    priority: 1  # 0f30
    parent:  # 0f30
    tags:  # 0f30
    - tracker  # 0f30
    - security  # 0f30
    - governance  # 0f30
    isbefore: []  # 0f30
    duplicate_of: []  # 0f30
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 0f30
    pr_ref:  # 0f30
    description: ""  # 0f30
    fields:  # 0f30
      statement: "The config.yaml governance boundary must not be transferable to the agent by an unprivileged agent-run command. Today handle_init (cli.py:1970) has no resolve_actor guard and calls config_lock(config_path) unconditionally, including on a config that already exists and is owned by the human. config_lock does path.chmod(0o444), which raises PermissionError for a non-owner, and falls back to _config_chmod_fallback -- which by its own docstring writes a copy that is now owned by the current user and renames it over the target. An agent running tracker init therefore ends up owning the file ADR-0013 says it must never be able to write."  # 0f30
      root_cause: "Two halves. (1) configure is correctly guarded (configure requires human authority, exits 1 before touching config_lock) but init is not, and init is the command that calls config_lock. (2) More fundamentally, the protection is file-level while the containing directory is not: .agent/tracker and .agent/tracker-workspace are mode 2775 group project-dev, and the agent is in project-dev, so the agent can create a file in those directories and rename it over the config regardless of the config's own owner or mode. ADR-0013's security model rests entirely on file ownership plus mode; a create-and-rename in a group-writable directory defeats both."  # 0f30
- op: discuss  # 0ab0
  at: '2026-09-12T17:36:01Z'  # 0ab0
  by: agent  # 0ab0
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 0ab0
  clock: 2  # 0ab0
  nonce: 0ab0  # 0ab0
  message: "OBSERVED STATE on this deployment 2026-09-12, which is what prompted the analysis:\n\n  .agent/tracker/config.yaml            mode 464  owner jgstern_agent:project-dev   <- agent-owned\n  .agent/tracker-workspace/config.yaml  mode 464  owner jgstern:project-dev\n  .agent/tracker/                       mode 2775 owner jgstern_agent:project-dev\n  .agent/tracker-workspace/             mode 2775 owner jgstern_agent:project-dev\n\nMode 464 is r--rw-r--: group-writable. Since the agent is in project-dev and is NOT the owner of the workspace config, the group bits apply there and the agent can write .agent/tracker-workspace/config.yaml directly today (os.access W_OK returns True). For .agent/tracker/config.yaml the agent IS the owner, so the r-- owner bits deny a direct write -- but an owner can always chmod its own file, so that denial is cosmetic.\n\nADR-0013 states the model twice and both statements are false here: line 100 says init sets chown <human_user> config.yaml && chmod 644 config.yaml and that \"the agent can read config (needs to, for validation) but cannot write it -- the OS enforces this\"; line 409 says \"the agent literally cannot write() to a file owned by the human user with mode 644\". ADR-0013 is ALSO stale on the number: the code targets 0o444 (config_lock), not 644.\n\nTHE SETUP WIZARD DETECTS ALL OF THIS AND CANNOT FIX IT FROM THE AGENT ACCOUNT, BY DESIGN. htrac setup (setup.py run_setup) is the idempotent wizard; _check_config_ownership and _check_config_permissions both branch on resolve_actor. Run as the human they auto-fix via os.chown / chmod. Run as the agent they return status=warn with the exact commands for the human. Invoked as the agent on this deployment they report:\n  config_ownership  [warn] config.yaml is owned by the agent user\n  config_permissions [warn] config.yaml is not read-only (0444)\nSo the operational remedy is for the human to run htrac setup from their own account, or the two commands it prints.\n\nI did NOT run the full wizard -- checks 16-21 are not read-only (wrapper shim, managed AGENTS.md block, core.hooksPath all write). I invoked only the two ownership/permission check functions, which are pure diagnostics in the agent branch.\n\nSEPARATE COSMETIC BUG IN THE SAME FUNCTION, worth fixing in the same PR: _check_config_ownership computes wrong_owner = [p for p in existing if p.stat().st_uid == current_uid] but its remediation detail line interpolates existing, not wrong_owner -- so it tells the human to chown BOTH configs when only one is wrong. The adjacent _check_config_permissions does it correctly (iterates wrong_mode). Harmless in effect (chown to the same owner is a no-op) but it hides which file is actually broken.\n\nPROPOSED FIX, not started, needs the owner because it touches the governance boundary itself: (a) guard handle_init with resolve_actor exactly as handle_configure is guarded; (b) fix the existing/wrong_owner interpolation; (c) correct ADR-0013 644 -> 444. (d) is the real design question and is the owner's: file-level ownership cannot protect a file in a group-writable directory, so either the ADR stops claiming OS enforcement, or the config moves outside the shared tree."  # 0ab0
- op: discuss  # baf2
  at: '2026-09-12T18:40:15Z'  # baf2
  by: agent  # baf2
  actor: jgstern_agent  # baf2
  clock: 3  # baf2
  nonce: baf2  # baf2
  message: "BUILT — PR #932, OPEN FOR HUMAN REVIEW (auto-pr --gov, CI green, deliberately NOT self-merged since it changes the governance boundary itself).\n\nOWNER RULING 2026-09-12: option B. Reasoning recorded because it defeated my recommendation: the dual-account architecture exists PRECISELY because the agent is not trusted, so \"this only pays off against a misbehaving agent\" describes the premise rather than a limitation. And hypergumbo-tracker is intended as a general utility, to be used with less friendly agents than the present one. My counter-argument (the agent can already change behaviour by PR-ing source) was sloppy: a source change passes through PR, CI and review; a config write passes through nothing. Not equivalent.\n\nWHAT SHIPPED. New module protected_config.py. Resolution order in load_config becomes: (0) /etc/hypergumbo-tracker/<12-hex>_<legible tail>/config.yaml -- authoritative when the host has opted in, in-repo file IGNORED entirely, never merged; (1) in-repo config.yaml; (2) template; (3) built-in defaults.\n\nTHE REQUIREMENT IS NARROWER THAN \"MOVE IT SOMEWHERE SAFE\", and getting this wrong would have broken the tracker: the agent MUST be able to READ the config (it validates every write against the status and kind vocabulary and resolves actor patterns from it). Putting it in the human home fails -- /home/jgstern is mode 750 and the agent cannot even traverse it (verified X_OK=False). The precise requirement is: agent reads; agent cannot write the file; agent cannot write the containing directory OR ANY PARENT. A root-owned 0755 directory holding a 0644 file satisfies all three.\n\nABSENCE IS FATAL, by design. A missing protected config does NOT fall through -- that is the downgrade attack, since an agent would then not need to write the protected file at all, only to stop it being found, and layer 1 is a file it fully controls. The EXISTENCE OF THE ROOT DIRECTORY is the opt-in signal: the agent can neither create nor remove /etc/hypergumbo-tracker, so it cannot forge either answer. Hosts that never create it are unaffected; single-account deployments are byte-for-byte unchanged.\n\nNO ENVIRONMENT OVERRIDE. The agent controls its own environment, so an env-redirectable root reopens the hole. The root is a parameter for tests only. Also: defaults resolve at CALL time, not definition time, so the value cannot silently diverge from PROTECTED_ROOT.\n\n/etc RATHER THAN THE HUMAN HOME because locating ~<human>/ requires knowing which user is the human, which is read from actor_resolution in the config being looked for. Circular. /etc needs no prior knowledge, and the sudo cost is already paid by any two-account deployment (groupadd/usermod/chgrp/chmod g+s).\n\nNAMING, per the owner tweak: <12-hex>_<legible path tail>, e.g. 8b85be80f37d_home_jgstern_agent_hypergumbo. Hash first because lookup GLOBS ON THE HASH PREFIX and treats the tail as a comment -- so an administrator may rename it to <hash>_prod without breaking resolution. Reconstructing the exact name would have made human annotation break lookup. Full path not basename (per-clone keying is the point); truncated from the LEFT so the informative tail survives; two directories claiming one hash is REFUSED, not guessed. Hash is journal._repo_id, imported not re-derived. The ops journal keeps a bare hash deliberately -- machine-managed, never hand-administered.\n\nSCOPE STATED RATHER THAN IMPLIED: an agent that relocates or re-clones the checkout gets a different id and falls through; it can also pip install its own tracker. Config protection cannot reach an agent running its own deployment. What it protects is THIS deployment governance record. Written into ADR-0013 rather than left to be discovered.\n\nALSO IN THIS PR: htrac setup gains check 10c, which prints the exact root commands with the legible directory name filled in (advisory only -- it never touches /etc, because a setup step that silently acquires root is its own governance problem). And the _check_config_ownership interpolation bug is fixed: it computed wrong_owner but printed existing, telling the human to chown every config rather than the broken one.\n\nADR-0013 AMENDED IN PLACE with a dated amendment note: the two OS-enforcement sentences corrected, the stale 644 corrected to 444, and a new Host-protected config section.\n\nGATES: 21 new tests, 2926 pass, 100% coverage in isolation, mypy ratchet 669 delta +0.\n\nSTILL OPEN and NOT done by this PR: handle_init still has no actor guard. It is the mechanism by which this deployment most likely lost the config in the first place, and it is a separate small fix."  # baf2
